                            Preamble

  The GNU Affero General Public License is a free, copyleft license for
software and other kinds of works, specifically designed to ensure
cooperation with the community in the case of network server software.

  The licenses for most software and other practical works are designed
to take away your freedom to share and change the works. By contrast,
our General Public Licenses are intended to guarantee your freedom to
share and change all versions of a program--to make sure it remains
free software for all its users. We, the Free Software Foundation, use
the GNU Affero General Public License for many of the licenses we
publish; we apply it, too, for any software we publish under the terms
of this AGPL, or any other free software license. When we speak of
free software, we are referring to freedom, not price. Our General
Public Licenses are designed to make sure that you have the freedom to
distribute copies of free software (and charge for them if you wish),
that you receive source code or can get it if you want it, that you
can change the software or use pieces of it in new free programs, and
that you know you can do these things.

  Developers that use our General Public Licenses protect your rights
with two steps: (1) assert copyright on the software, and (2) offer
you this License which gives you legal permission to copy, distribute
and/or modify the software.

  A secondary benefit of defending every contributor's rights is that
it inspires other contributors to continue developing and free software
contributing their own improvements to it.

  The GPL is designed to ensure that every user of the program can
obtain the source code; the GPL assures that the users of a program
receive the source code or are offered it on the same terms as the
authors. The AGPL differs from the GPL in that the conditions for
modifying and distributing the program are also extended to the
interactions of the program over a network. Network use of the program
constitutes a form of distribution of the program.

  For the developers' and authors' protection, the GPL clearly explains
that there is no warranty for this free software. For both authors'
and users' sake, the GPL requires that modified versions be marked as
changed, so that their problems will not be attributed erroneously to
authors of previous versions.

  Some devices are designed to deny users access to install or run
modified versions of the software inside them, although the maker can
do so. This is fundamentally incompatible with the aim of protecting
users' freedom to change the software. The systematic pattern of such
abuse occurs in the area of products for people who will use them
in personal or organizational activities, which is precisely where it
is most needed. Therefore, we have designed this version of the GPL to
prohibit the practice for those who wish to exercise their rights and
prohibit it from being a problem.

  Finally, every program is threatened constantly by software patents.
States should not allow patents to restrict development and use of
software on general-purpose computers, but in those that do, we wish
to avoid the special danger that patents applied to a program could
make it effectively proprietary. To prevent this, the GPL assures that
patents cannot be used to render the program non-free.

  The precise terms and conditions for copying, distribution and
modification follow.

